---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import RandomPicker from '@/components/tools/everyday/RandomPicker.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="random-picker">
  <RandomPicker client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to pick a random winner</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Paste your list into the box, one entry per line. The random picker ignores blank lines and
        removes duplicate lines unless you turn that off.
      </li>
      <li>Choose how many winners you want. Winners are always distinct: nobody is drawn twice.</li>
      <li>
        Press <strong>Pick</strong>. Winners appear one at a time, and you can copy the full result as a
        numbered list.
      </li>
      <li>
        Switch to <strong>Shuffle</strong> to put the entire list in a random order, for example for a
        running order, seating, review rotas or random task assignment.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Weighted draws</h2>
    <p>
      Turn on weights and add a number after a vertical bar to change an entrant's odds:
    </p>
    <pre><code>Alice | 3
Bob | 1
Charlie | 2</code></pre>
    <p>
      Alice has three chances in six, Charlie two and Bob one. The odds table lists each entrant's exact
      probability of being drawn first. Use weights for raffle tickets bought in different quantities,
      entries earned by activity, or a rota where one person is due a larger share.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you draw several winners with weights, each pick removes that entrant from the pool and the
      remaining probabilities are recalculated. This is the standard weighted sampling-without-replacement
      procedure.
    </p>

    <h2>How the randomness works</h2>
    <p>
      A fair draw needs a good source of randomness and a correct algorithm.
    </p>
    <p>
      The source is <code>crypto.getRandomValues</code>, the browser's cryptographically secure
      generator, with rejection sampling so that each index is exactly equally likely. There is no modulo
      bias.
    </p>
    <p>
      The algorithm is a Fisher–Yates shuffle. It walks the list from the end and swaps each item with a
      uniformly chosen earlier one, which gives every possible order with equal probability in linear
      time. The common shortcut <code>array.sort(() =&gt; Math.random() - 0.5)</code> is measurably
      biased: comparison sorts expect a consistent order, and a random comparator does not give one. Some
      orders come up several times more often than others.
    </p>

    <h2>Running a giveaway people will accept</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Freeze the entry list first. Export it, note how many entries there are, and do not edit it after
        the deadline.
      </li>
      <li>
        Decide the rules before you draw: how many winners, what happens if a winner does not respond,
        and whether duplicate entries are allowed.
      </li>
      <li>
        Draw substitutes at the same time. Pick five winners when you need three, and publish the order.
      </li>
      <li>
        Record the draw. A screen recording with the entry count visible is a practical substitute for an
        auditor.
      </li>
      <li>
        The draw runs only in your browser, so there is no third-party log to use as an audit trail. A
        legally regulated prize draw may need a verifiable service.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Odds at a glance</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Entries</th>
            <th scope="col">1 winner</th>
            <th scope="col">3 winners</th>
            <th scope="col">10 winners</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>10</td><td>10%</td><td>30%</td><td>100%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50</td><td>2%</td><td>6%</td><td>20%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100</td><td>1%</td><td>3%</td><td>10%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>500</td><td>0.2%</td><td>0.6%</td><td>2%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1,000</td><td>0.1%</td><td>0.3%</td><td>1%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10,000</td><td>0.01%</td><td>0.03%</td><td>0.1%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      With equal weights and a draw without replacement, each entrant's chance of being among the winners
      is winners ÷ entries.
    </p>

    <h2>Other uses</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Picking who presents first, or the order of a stand-up</li>
      <li>Making secret-santa pairings by shuffling twice</li>
      <li>Choosing a random sample of records for a spot check</li>
      <li>Splitting a class into random groups: shuffle, then deal off the top</li>
      <li>Making a decision with more than two options</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Entrant names, emails and handles stay in this browser tab, which matters when the list holds
      customer data or colleagues' names.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
